<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imre Nagy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imre Nagy was a Hungarian communist who as prime minister from 1953 to 1955 eased Stalinist repression before being ousted. During the revolution of October 1956 he returned to power, accepted a multi-party system and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s neutrality and withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a>. Soviet forces crushed the revolution, and he was secretly tried and executed in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imre-nagy.jpg" alt="Imre Nagy" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ismeretlen szerző(Nagy Imre Emlékház tulajdona) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imre_Nagy_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Imre Nagy was born on 7 June 1896 in Kaposvár, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, to a family of peasant origin. His father was a carriage driver and later an unskilled worker. After a poor record at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> he trained as a locksmith and metal fitter.</p><p>Conscripted into the Austro-Hungarian Army in 1914, he was wounded on the Italian Front and again on the Eastern Front, where Russian forces took him prisoner in July 1916. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> he joined a Marxist discussion group, and in 1918 he joined the Communist Party of the Foreign Workers of Siberia and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. He joined the Russian Communist Party in 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent by the Hungarian Communist Party to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1921, Nagy worked on underground networks and was arrested several times. From 1930 he lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, where he did agricultural research and, from 1933 to 1941, served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> as an informer under the codename Volodia.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and was Minister of Agriculture, distributing land to the peasants, then Interior Minister. As prime minister from 1953 to 1955 he promoted his New Course, easing the harshest parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi">Mátyás Rákosi</a>'s Stalinist regime, but Rákosi's influence prevailed and he was dismissed on 18 April 1955, losing his party posts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s Secret Speech of February 1956, criticism of Rákosi grew, and he was forced out in July. On 23 October a student demonstration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> called for Nagy's return, and the revolt began. Nagy was named prime minister on 24 October. On 28 October he negotiated a ceasefire with Soviet representatives and announced the dissolution of the ÁVH secret police.</p><p>By 30 October he promised a multi-party system, and on 1 November he announced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> and appealed to the UN for recognition of its neutrality. Soviet forces attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 4 November, and Nagy took refuge in the Yugoslav Embassy.</p><p>Despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">János Kádár</a>'s written safe conduct, he was arrested on 22 November as he left the embassy and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He was secretly tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for treason and the organisation of the overthrow of the republic, and hanged on 16 June 1958; his burial was in an unmarked grave in the prison yard. In June 1989 he and other figures of the revolution were rehabilitated and reburied with full honours, an event that played a key part in the fall of the Hungarian Socialist Workers' Party regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imre Nagy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hungarian Revolution of 1956 was crushed by more than 150,000 Soviet troops; nearly 20,000 Hungarians died and <b>Imre Nagy</b> was hanged in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet forces launched a massive assault on Budapest at dawn, overthrowing <b>Imre Nagy</b>'s government.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Stalin's death <b>Imre Nagy</b> eased repression, and on 23 October 1956 a popular revolution brought him back to power and declared neutrality.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kaposvár, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.37,17.78&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Kaposvár on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Imre Nagy"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4284 6089h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4284 6089h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.37,17.78&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
